      "id": 1,
      "text": "Fallon, Montana earned its name from Benjamin O'Fallon, a nephew of Captain William Clark and early Indian agent.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Fallon_County%2C_Montana",
      "category": "etymology",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Northern Pacific Railroad opened a station in Fallon in 1883, and Cyrus Mendenhall became its first postmaster the next year.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/127702",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Fallon sits on the edge of the scenic badlands and is known for its local moss agate deposits.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.visitmt.com/cities-towns/fallon",
      "category": "nature",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the late 1910s and early 1920s, Fallon boasted two grain elevators, a bank, and a newspaper serving ranchers.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.mtmemory.org/nodes/view/127702",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Today, ranching and farming remain the backbone of Fallon’s economy, supporting the local community and sustaining its rural character.",
